# Process Validation Rule

## Purpose & Scope

Defines comprehensive process validation standards for software development workflows. This rule ensures development processes follow best practices, maintain quality, and meet organizational standards across the entire software development lifecycle.

**Applies to**: All development processes, workflows, and documentation before code review, merge, or deployment

**Does not apply to**: Individual code implementations (covered by other validation rules)

## Inputs (Contract)

- Development process documentation
- Workflow configurations
- CI/CD pipeline definitions
- Project management artifacts
- Quality assurance procedures
- Release management processes

## Outputs (Contract)

- Process validation report
- Workflow efficiency analysis
- Quality gate compliance
- Process improvement recommendations
- Process validation checklist
- Process documentation audit

## Core Process Principles

**PROCESS IS THE FOUNDATION OF QUALITY - Good processes lead to good outcomes.**

**CONTINUOUS IMPROVEMENT - Processes should evolve based on feedback.**

**TRANSPARENCY AND TRACEABILITY - Every action should be documented and traceable.**

**COLLABORATION AND COMMUNICATION - Processes should facilitate teamwork.**

## Mandatory Process Gates

### Gate 1: Development Process Compliance (CRITICAL)

#### Software Development Lifecycle (SDLC)
- [ ] SDLC phases properly defined
- [ ] Requirements gathering complete
- [ ] Design specifications complete
- [ ] Implementation follows standards
- [ ] Testing comprehensive
- [ ] Deployment procedures defined

#### Agile Methodology Compliance
- [ ] Sprint planning completed
- [ ] User stories properly defined
- [ ] Backlog management effective
- [ ] Daily standups conducted
- [ ] Sprint reviews completed
- [ ] Retrospectives conducted

#### Project Management Compliance
- [ ] Project plans complete
- [ ] Resource allocation proper
- [ ] Timeline management effective
- [ ] Risk management implemented
- [ ] Stakeholder communication maintained

### Gate 2: Quality Assurance and Testing (CRITICAL)

#### Testing Strategy
- [ ] Unit testing implemented
- [ ] Integration testing complete
- [ ] System testing performed
- [ ] User acceptance testing complete
- [ ] Performance testing conducted

#### Quality Gates
- [ ] Code review requirements met
- [ ] Test coverage requirements met
- [ ] Security testing completed
- [ ] Performance testing completed
- [ ] Documentation complete

#### Quality Metrics
- [ ] Defect density within acceptable limits
- [ ] Test pass rate meets requirements
- [ ] Code coverage meets requirements
- [ ] Performance benchmarks met
- [ ] Security scan clean

### Gate 3: CI/CD and Automation (HIGHLY RECOMMENDED)

#### Continuous Integration
- [ ] Automated builds implemented
- [ ] Automated testing in CI
- [ ] Code quality checks automated
- [ ] Security scanning automated
- [ ] Build artifacts properly managed

#### Continuous Deployment
- [ ] Automated deployment pipelines
- [ ] Environment promotion proper
- [ ] Rollback procedures defined
- [ ] Monitoring implemented
- [ ] Alerting configured

#### Infrastructure as Code
- [ ] Infrastructure defined in code
- [ ] Configuration management proper
- [ ] Environment consistency maintained
- [ ] Infrastructure testing implemented
- [ ] Infrastructure monitoring configured

### Gate 4: Documentation and Communication (HIGHLY RECOMMENDED)

#### Process Documentation
- [ ] Process documentation complete
- [ ] Workflow documentation proper
- [ ] Decision tracking implemented
- [ ] Knowledge sharing effective
- [ ] Documentation updated regularly

#### Communication Channels
- [ ] Team communication effective
- [ ] Stakeholder communication proper
- [ ] Documentation accessible
- [ ] Information sharing efficient
- [ ] Feedback loops established

#### Collaboration Tools
- [ ] Project management tools used
- [ ] Version control proper
- [ ] Documentation tools effective
- [ ] Communication tools integrated
- [ ] Collaboration processes defined

